"""Plain-language terms; build.py adds links and writes static glossary HTML."""
TERMS = [
 ('explanation','Explanation','A claim that a model component (neuron, direction, head, feature) responds to, or computes, something nameable. Until tested it is a hypothesis.'),
 ('demonstrated-explanation','Demonstrated explanation','An explanation that predicted the component’s behaviour on data chosen before the look, beat its controls, and met a threshold fixed in advance.'),
 ('pre-registration','Pre-registration','Writing the prediction, the data it will be scored on, the scoring function, the controls, and the pass threshold before running the test.'),
 ('prediction-test','Prediction test','A procedure that turns an explanation into a number on held-out data: for example the correlation between activations predicted from the explanation and the activations actually observed.'),
 ('held-out','Held-out data','Inputs that played no part in forming or choosing the explanation. Scores on them are the only ones that cannot be inflated by selection.'),
 ('screening-split','Screening split','The portion of data used to search for candidates and form hypotheses. Any score computed on it is biased upward by the search.'),
 ('control','Control','A comparison that would also pass if the claim were empty: a random neuron, shuffled labels, a baseline explanation, a rotated stimulus. The claim must beat its controls.'),
 ('random-neuron-control','Random-neuron control','Scoring the same explanation against randomly chosen other units. It measures how much of the score comes from the explanation fitting the data rather than the unit.'),
 ('shuffled-label-control','Shuffled-label control','Scoring the unit against a permutation of the explanation’s predictions. It gives the distribution of scores a meaningless explanation would get.'),
 ('selection-bias','Selection bias','The upward distortion of a score when the thing scored was chosen because it scored well. Searching many units and reporting the best one is the common case.'),
 ('neuron','Neuron','One coordinate of an activation vector; in a transformer MLP, one hidden unit after its nonlinearity; in a convolutional network, one channel of a feature map.'),
 ('native-neuron','Native neuron','A neuron of the network as trained, studied directly, as opposed to a learned feature produced by a second model such as a sparse autoencoder.'),
 ('direction','Direction','A unit vector in activation space. Projecting activations onto it gives a scalar; a probe or a feature is a direction with a claim attached.'),
 ('residual-stream','Residual stream','The vector each transformer block reads from and adds to; the sum of the embeddings and every block’s attention and MLP outputs so far.'),
 ('attention-head','Attention head','One of several parallel units in an attention layer, each computing a pattern of weights over earlier positions and moving information along it.'),
 ('attention-pattern','Attention pattern','The matrix of weights a head assigns from each query position to each key position; rows sum to one.'),
 ('mlp','MLP','The feed-forward block of a transformer layer: a linear map up to a wider hidden layer, a nonlinearity, and a linear map back.'),
 ('activation','Activation','The value of a unit on a given input. For a neuron, a scalar per token or per image position.'),
 ('polysemantic','Polysemantic neuron','A neuron that responds to several apparently unrelated inputs. Whether a given case is truly unrelated, or one concept nobody has named, is an empirical question.'),
 ('monosemantic','Monosemantic','Responding to one nameable thing. A claim of monosemanticity needs the same prediction test as any other explanation.'),
 ('superposition','Superposition','The hypothesis that a network represents more features than it has neurons by spreading features across neurons, so single neurons mix features.'),
 ('feature','Feature','A direction or unit claimed to correspond to one meaningful property of the input. Used both for native neurons and for learned dictionary elements.'),
 ('sparse-autoencoder','Sparse autoencoder','A second model trained to reconstruct activations from a sparse, overcomplete set of learned directions. Its latents are offered as more interpretable features than neurons.'),
 ('sae-feature','SAE feature','One latent of a sparse autoencoder: a direction and a sparse activation. Its explanation is scored the same way a neuron’s is.'),
 ('max-activating-examples','Max-activating examples','The inputs on which a unit fires most strongly. They are the first thing to look at and the easiest thing to be misled by.'),
 ('quantile-sampling','Quantile sampling','Drawing random examples from fixed bands of the unit’s activation distribution, so that weak and typical activations are seen as well as the strongest.'),
 ('simulation-scoring','Simulation scoring','Having a simulator (a model or a rule) predict a unit’s activation on each token from the explanation alone, then scoring the predictions against the true activations.'),
 ('explanation-score','Explanation score','The correlation between simulated and true activations, as used by Bills et al. (2023). Its value depends heavily on which text excerpts it is computed on.'),
 ('correlation','Correlation','The Pearson coefficient between two series: 1 means a perfect linear match, 0 no linear relation, −1 a perfect inverse match.'),
 ('precision-recall','Precision and recall','For an explanation read as a predicate: precision is the fraction of inputs matching the predicate on which the unit fires; recall is the fraction of inputs where the unit fires that match the predicate.'),
 ('downstream-kl','Downstream KL','The divergence between the model’s output distribution with a unit’s true activations and with simulated ones substituted. It measures whether the simulation errors matter to the model.'),
 ('feature-visualization','Feature visualization','Optimising an input image by gradient ascent to maximise a unit’s activation. Shows what excites the unit; does not show how often that occurs in real data.'),
 ('synthetic-stimulus','Synthetic stimulus','A rendered input with known parameters (orientation, radius, contrast) used to measure a unit’s response as a function of those parameters.'),
 ('tuning-curve','Tuning curve','A unit’s response plotted against one stimulus parameter, such as orientation.'),
 ('orientation-selectivity','Orientation selectivity','How concentrated a tuning curve is around one orientation; the course uses the vector strength of the response over the circle, 0 flat to 1 single-angle.'),
 ('linear-probe','Linear probe','A classifier fitted on activations to predict a label. High held-out accuracy shows the label is linearly readable from the activations; it does not show the model uses it.'),
 ('logit-lens','Logit lens','Applying the model’s final layer norm and unembedding to the residual stream after an intermediate block, to read what the block’s state would predict if it were the last.'),
 ('direct-logit-attribution','Direct logit attribution','Decomposing a logit into the contributions of each component’s output projected onto the target token’s unembedding row, through the final layer norm.'),
 ('activation-patching','Activation patching','Running a corrupted input while replacing one activation with its value from a clean input, to measure how much of the clean behaviour that activation restores.'),
 ('ablation','Ablation','Removing a component’s contribution (setting it to zero or to a mean) and measuring the change in the output.'),
 ('correlational-evidence','Correlational evidence','Evidence that a unit’s activity co-varies with a property. It supports “represents”; it does not support “is used for”.'),
 ('interventional-evidence','Interventional evidence','Evidence from changing a unit and observing the output. It supports causal claims about what the unit does for the model.'),
 ('clean-corrupted-pair','Clean/corrupted pair','Two inputs that differ in one respect the claim is about, with different expected outputs, used for patching.'),
 ('induction-head','Induction head','An attention head that, on a repeated sequence, attends from a token to the token that followed its earlier occurrence and raises that token’s logit: the rule [A][B]…[A] → [B].'),
 ('prefix-matching-score','Prefix-matching score','On a repeated random sequence, the mean attention from each second-copy token to the token after its first-copy occurrence.'),
 ('in-context-learning','In-context learning','A model predicting later tokens better because of earlier tokens in the same context, measured by the drop in loss along the sequence.'),
 ('threshold','Threshold','The pass line fixed before the test. A threshold chosen after seeing the result is not a threshold.'),
 ('base-rate','Base rate','How often the property the explanation names occurs in the data. It bounds what accuracy and correlation can achieve and must be reported beside them.'),
 ('negative-result','Negative result','A pre-registered test that failed. Reported with the same detail as a success, it is a contribution; hidden, it is the reason the field cannot trust the successes.'),
 ('multiple-comparisons','Multiple comparisons','Testing many candidates and reporting the best. The best of n meaningless scores is not meaningless-looking; held-out data and corrected thresholds are the remedies.'),
 ('receptive-field','Receptive field','The region of the input that can influence one position of a convolutional feature map.'),
 ('transformerlens','TransformerLens','A library that loads transformer models with named hook points so activations can be cached and edited; version 4 boots models through TransformerBridge.'),
 ('hook-point','Hook point','A named location in the forward pass where an activation can be read or replaced, such as blocks.5.hook_resid_post.'),
 ('cache','Activation cache','A dictionary from hook-point names to the activations recorded during one forward pass.'),
 ('attribution-graph','Attribution graph','A diagram of which components contributed to an output, built by tracing contributions backward from the output through the network.'),
 ('replication','Replication','Repeating a published test on new data, a new model copy, or new stimuli, with the test fixed in advance, and reporting what did and did not hold.'),
]
GLOSSARY = {slug: dict(name=name, short=defn.split('. ')[0].rstrip('.')+'.', defn=defn) for slug, name, defn in TERMS}
